public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * Applies the standard discount to new OpportunityLineItem records when
     * Discount is null. Existing discount values, including 0, are preserved.
     *
     * Note: This deliberately avoids calling PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)
     * inside the record loop because that method performs a SOQL query per call.
     * With CPQ quotes of up to 200 line items, that can exceed the 100 SOQL query
     * governor limit.
     */
    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLineItems) {
        if (newLineItems == null || newLineItems.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Collect only the Product2 records whose line items need a discount.
        Set<Id> productIdsToDiscount = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLineItems) {
            if (
                line != null
                && line.Discount == null
                && line.Product2Id != null
            ) {
                productIdsToDiscount.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (productIdsToDiscount.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Bulk query needed Product2 pricing input.
        Map<Id, String> productFamilyById = new Map<Id, String>();

        for (Product2 product : [
            SELECT Family
            FROM Product2
            WHERE Id IN :productIdsToDiscount
        ]) {
            productFamilyById.put(product.Id, product.Family);
        }

        // Cache discount by family so PricingRules is not called repeatedly
        // for the same family.
        Map<String, Decimal> discountByFamily = new Map<String, Decimal>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLineItems) {
            if (
                line != null
                && line.Discount == null
                && line.Product2Id != null
            ) {
                String family = productFamilyById.get(line.Product2Id);

                // Unknown family or missing product falls back to the pricing
                // rules' default behavior for an unmatched family.
                if (family == null) {
                    family = '';
                }

                if (!discountByFamily.containsKey(family)) {
                    discountByFamily.put(
                        family,
                        PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)
                    );
                }

                line.Discount = discountByFamily.get(family);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## What I’d change and why

I would **not** call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` for each line inside the trigger loop.

The reason is that `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` contains a SOQL query:

```apex
Product2 product = [SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId];
```

So calling it once per line item creates a SOQL query once per line item. A CPQ quote with 200 lines could become 200 queries, which is unsafe because Apex has a per-transaction limit of 100 queries. Even when 100 or fewer records are processed, this is also slower and less predictable.

The implementation above keeps the pricing rules in one place by using `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(...)`, but it bulk-queries the needed `Product2` families first. It does not copy the discount logic into the trigger handler.

One caveat: today `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` is only family-based, so using `standardDiscountForFamily` is equivalent. If the pricing team later makes discounts product-specific rather than family-specific, the better change would be for them to expose a bulk-friendly method such as `standardDiscountFor(List<Id>)`. The downstream handler should then call that bulk method instead of duplicating the rule lookup.

